  <header class="hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">App &amp; Feature Audit · Brand Method</span>
    <h1>Khayr, <em>App &amp; Feature Audit</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">A dua card deck in one self-contained file, now carrying two utility features: waktu solat for every JAKIM zone and a qibla compass that can calibrate itself from the sun. This audit reads the new build line by line, verifies its claims against the live JAKIM API and the mathematics, and names the four gaps between a working feature and a competitive one.</p>
    <div class="vrow"><span class="vchip">Audited 5 Aug 2026</span><span class="vchip">60 JAKIM zones</span><span class="vchip">Bearing verified: KL 292.5°</span><span class="vchip">0 console errors</span></div>
  </header>

  <div class="execsum">
    <span class="eyebrow">The verdict, in 60 seconds</span>
    <h2 style="margin-top:12px;max-width:34ch">Ship-grade engineering, hidden behind a three-dot menu.</h2>
    <p class="sec-intro">The new build is honest work. Official times come straight from <strong>e-solat.gov.my</strong> for all 60 zones, a week at a time, cached for offline. Where there is no zone, a solar calculation takes over, with five methods and both Asr rulings. The qibla bearing is correct to the published figure, and when a device has no compass the feature says so and offers the sun instead of pretending. What remains is surface, not substance: the sheets speak English only, the reader must find their own zone in a list of 60, and both features live behind the overflow menu where no first-time reader will look.</p>
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="card"><span class="tag">Verified</span><h3>The times are real</h3><p>Live fetch against zone SGR01 and WLY01 returned official JAKIM times matching the API byte for byte, Hijri date included.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="tag">Verified</span><h3>The maths is right</h3><p>Great-circle bearing from Kuala Lumpur computes 292.5° from true north. JAKIM publishes 291° 7′ for the neighbouring zone reference point. Agreement within the expected margin.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><span class="tag">Gap</span><h3>Nobody will find it</h3><p>Both features sit behind the ··· menu. Every competitor puts the next prayer on the first screen. Surface it, or it may as well not exist.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chapdiv" id="c1"><div class="cd-no">01</div><div><div class="cd-h">The product today</div><div class="cd-sub">What one file contains</div></div></div>
  <section>
    <p class="sec-intro"><strong>Khayr.html is the whole app.</strong> Thirty verified dua cards in four sets, three ways to hold the deck, English and Bahasa Melayu, saves with an honest free cap, a premium tier that only sells what exists, and a content pipeline that refuses hand-typed Arabic. The August update adds roughly 1,000 lines: a prayer-times sheet, a qibla compass with camera view, and the plumbing both need.</p>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">30</div><div class="lab">Dua cards</div><div class="s">4 sets, all source-keyed</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">60</div><div class="lab">JAKIM zones</div><div class="s">every state, grouped</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">26</div><div class="lab">World cities</div><div class="s">calculated fallback</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">5</div><div class="lab">Calc methods</div><div class="s">JAKIM, MWL, Egypt, Karachi, Umm al-Qura</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="num">1</div><div class="lab">File</div><div class="s">4,026 lines, no build step</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="chapdiv" id="c2"><div class="cd-no">02</div><div><div class="cd-h">Waktu solat, line by line</div><div class="cd-sub">What was checked and what was found</div></div></div>
  <section>
    <div class="tscroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Check</th><th style="width:90px">Verdict</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="dim">Official JAKIM times</td><td>Fetched live from e-solat.gov.my for SGR01 and WLY01. CORS header is open, JSON parsed cleanly, rendered times match the API response exactly. Today, 5 August: Subuh 06:01, Zohor 13:22, Asar 16:42, Maghrib 19:29, Isyak 20:41.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">All locations</td><td>All 60 e-solat zones present, grouped by state in the picker, from JHR01 to WLY02. Zone choice persists in localStorage under baik.ptz.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Offline continuity</td><td>One fetch stores a full week per zone. The sheet keeps working with no connection, and tops the week up when it can. A failed fetch backs off for 60 seconds instead of spinning.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Anywhere else on earth</td><td>Without a zone, times are found by bisecting the solar altitude curve: one solar model serves both the compass and the clock, so the two can never disagree. Five methods, standard and Hanafi Asr. Polar edge case handled: an angle the sun never reaches reports itself instead of inventing a time.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">The countdown</td><td>Next prayer with ring countdown, updated in place once a second so rebuilds cannot restart the animation. After Isyak it rolls to tomorrow's Subuh from the cached week. When the time arrives it says so for ninety seconds, then moves on.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Clock discipline</td><td>The Malaysian calendar day is computed at UTC+8 regardless of the device clock, and e-solat dates arrive in Malay: Ogos parses, and so does the English spelling. The Hijri date comes from JAKIM itself, 21 Safar 1448 today.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">App view</td><td>In the phone layout the sheet takes its real phone shape, bottom-anchored at 392px. Zone choice carries across views.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <span class="pin"><b>Why the JAKIM fetch matters:</b> calculation and JAKIM agree within a minute or two on everything except Subuh, where the official table sits about ten minutes later. Apps that only calculate are quietly wrong in Malaysia five times a day. Khayr takes the official number and keeps the calculation as the fallback, which is the correct order.</span>
  </section>

  <div class="chapdiv" id="c3"><div class="cd-no">03</div><div><div class="cd-h">Qibla, line by line</div><div class="cd-sub">The compass, the sun, and the camera</div></div></div>
  <section>
    <div class="tscroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Check</th><th style="width:90px">Verdict</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="dim">The bearing</td><td>Great-circle initial bearing to the Kaaba at 21.4225°N 39.8262°E. Kuala Lumpur computes 292.54°, Kuching 292.12°, Kota Kinabalu 290.62°, all against true north. JAKIM's published bearing for the KL zone is 291° 7′ from its own reference point. The formula is right.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Honest sensors</td><td>On a device with no magnetometer the dial does not pretend. It says no reading came through and offers the sun method. This is rarer than it should be.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Sun calibration</td><td>Solar azimuth by NOAA's short form, good to a fraction of a degree, needs no sensor at all. Point the phone at the sun, and true north follows. No competitor audited offers this.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Camera view</td><td>The qibla laid over what the lens sees, with a ground line from your feet toward Makkah. Distance shown in km.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Built</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">Fallback location</td><td>Twenty-six cities from Kuala Lumpur to New York for readers who decline the location prompt, plus manual coordinates.</td><td><span class="vtag hi">Verified</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </section>

  <div class="chapdiv" id="c4"><div class="cd-no">04</div><div><div class="cd-h">The gaps</div><div class="cd-sub">Four findings, none structural</div></div></div>
  <section>
    <div class="tscroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Finding</th><th>Repair</th><th style="width:90px">Severity</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="dim">1</td><td>Both features live only behind the ··· overflow menu. The deck's readers will never discover them, and a prayer-times feature that is not glanceable has no reason to exist.</td><td>A next-prayer chip on the masthead in both layouts: name, time, countdown. One tap opens the sheet.</td><td><span class="vtag bad">High</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">2</td><td>The sheet chrome speaks English only. Prayer names carry BM inline, but Today, Next prayer, the zone notes and every button ignore the EN | BM toggle that the rest of the app honours.</td><td>Route all sheet strings through the existing LANG switch. The pattern is already in the codebase.</td><td><span class="vtag lo">Medium</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">3</td><td>Use my location does not pick the reader's JAKIM zone. It produces calculated times, while the official table sits one manual pick away in a list of 60.</td><td>A static zone-to-coordinates table plus nearest-neighbour match, so location lands on the official times with consent to a rough guess.</td><td><span class="vtag lo">Medium</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="dim">4</td><td>No azan notification. A browser tab cannot reliably wake a phone, so the web version cannot honestly promise one.</td><td>Do not fake it with unreliable web push. State it plainly, and carry it as the flagship reason for the native port.</td><td><span class="vtag lo">Noted</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <span class="pin warn"><b>One dependency to watch:</b> e-solat.gov.my is a single government endpoint with no SLA. The week cache absorbs short outages, and the calculation absorbs long ones, so the design already degrades gracefully. If it ever hardens its CORS policy, a small proxy becomes the fix.</span>
  </section>

  <div class="chapdiv" id="c5"><div class="cd-no">05</div><div><div class="cd-h">Verdict</div><div class="cd-sub">Where this leaves the product</div></div></div>
  <section>
    <p class="sec-intro">The hard parts are done and done well: official data, honest fallbacks, correct mathematics, offline behaviour, accessibility scaffolding. The four gaps are all surface work, and all four are cheap relative to what was just built. Fix discoverability first: it converts an engineering achievement into a daily habit. Then BM, then the zone auto-pick. The notification question belongs to the native port and should be answered there, not faked here. The full sequencing lives in the <a href="/plan" style="color:var(--ink);font-weight:600">Plan</a>.</p>
  </section>

